   <div class=\"fact-card\">\n                    <div class=\"fact-label\">\n                        Contains\n                    </div>\n\n                    <h3>\n        <a href=\"/bhopal-disaster/bhopal-disaster-entry-11\" class=\"entity-link\">\n            Bhopal disaster entry 11\n        </a>\n    </h3>\n\n                    <p>Supporting entry under Bhopal disaster.</p>\n\n                    \n\n                    \n                </div>\n            \n                <div class=\"fact-card\">\n                    <div class=\"fact-label\">\n                        Contains\n                    </div>\n\n                    <h3>\n        <a href=\"/bhopal-disaster/bhopal-disaster-entry-12\" class=\"entity-link\">\n            Bhopal disaster entry 12\n        </a>\n    </h3>\n\n                    <p>Supporting entry under Bhopal disaster.</p>\n\n                    \n\n                    \n                </div>\n            \n                <div class=\"fact-card\">\n                    <div class=\"fact-label\">\n                        Contains\n                    </div>\n\n                    <h3>\n        <a href=\"/bhopal-disaster/bhopal-disaster-entry-13\" class=\"entity-link\">\n            Bhopal disaster entry 13\n        </a>\n    </h3>\n\n                    <p>Supporting entry under Bhopal disaster.</p>\n\n                    \n\n                    \n                </div>\n            \n                <div class=\"fact-card\">\n                    <div class=\"fact-label\">\n                        Contains\n                    </div>\n\n                    <h3>\n        <a href=\"/bhopal-disaster/bhopal-disaster-entry-14\" class=\"entity-link\">\n            Bhopal disaster entry 14\n        </a>\n    </h3>\n\n                    <p>Supporting entry under Bhopal disaster.</p>\n\n                    \n\n                    \n                </div>\n            \n        </div>\n    </details>\n</section>\n\n</section>\n\n\n<section class=\"did-you-know\">\n    <h2>Did you know?</h2>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Figures are the primary trailheads for this subject graph.</li><li>Bhopal disaster sources is a primary trailhead under Bhopal disaster.</li><li>Bhopal disaster geography is a primary trailhead under Bhopal disaster.</li><li>Bhopal disaster legacy is a primary trailhead under Bhopal disaster.</li><li>Evidence around Bhopal disaster is classified as documented on Ton-o-Lore.</li><li>Bhopal disaster currently maps to 39 linked entities inside Bhopal disaster.</li>\n    </ul>\n</section>\n\n\n<section class=\"sources\">\n    <h2>Sources &amp; further reading</h2>\n    <p><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bhopal_disaster\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Fandom wiki</a></p>\n</section>","overview":"<section class=\"overview\">\n    <h2>What is Bhopal disaster?</h2>\n    \n    <p>\n        On 3 December 1984, over 500,000 people in the vicinity of the Union Carbide India Limited pesticide plant in Bhopal, Madhya Pradesh, India, were exposed to the highly toxic gas methyl isocyanate, in what is considered the world&#039;s worst industrial disaster.*\n    </p>\n    <p>\n        The owner of the factory, Union Carbide India Limited (UCIL), was a publicly-traded company which was majority-owned and controlled by the UCC, while the remaining 49.1 percent stake was held by the Indian public and Indian government-controlled banks. In 1994, UCC sold its stake in UCIL to Eveready Industries India Limited (EIIL), which subsequently merged with McLeod Russel (India) Ltd. Eveready ended clean-up on the site in 1998, when it terminated its 99-year lease and turned over control of the site to the state government of Madhya Pradesh. Dow Chemical Company purchased UCC in 2001, seventeen years after the disaster.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n        Civil and criminal cases filed in the United States against UCC and Warren Anderson, chief executive officer of the UCC at the time of the disaster, were dismissed and redirected to Indian courts on multiple occasions between 1986 and 2012, as the US courts focused on UCIL being a standalone entity of India. Civil and criminal cases were also filed in the District Court of Bhopal, India, involving UCC, UCIL, and Anderson. In June 2010, seven Indian nationals who were UCIL employees in 1984, including the former UCIL chairman Keshub Mahindra, were convicted in Bhopal of causing death by negligence and sentenced to two years&#039; imprisonment and a fine of about $2,000 each, the maximum punishment allowed by Indian law. All were released on bail shortly after the verdict. An eighth former employee was also convicted, but died before the judgement was passed.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n        The UCIL factory was built in 1969 to produce the pesticide Sevin (UCC&#039;s brand name for carbaryl) using methyl isocyanate (MIC) as an intermediate. An MIC production plant was added to the UCIL site in 1979. The chemical process employed in the Bhopal plant had methylamine reacting with phosgene to form MIC, which was in turn reacted with 1-naphthol to form the final product, carbaryl. Another manufacturer, Bayer, also used this MIC-intermediate process at the chemical plant once owned by UCC at Institute, West Virginia in the United States.\n    </p>\n    \n</section>","word_count":1186,"uniqueness_score":"1.0000"},"entity":{"id":"c99961a5-053d-412f-89b4-681e26ee9908","name":"Bhopal disaster","slug":"bhopal-disaster","type":"topic","kind":"topic","type_label":"Topic","short_description":"On 3 December 1984, over 500,000 people in the vicinity of the Union Carbide India Limited pesticide plant in Bhopal, Madhya Pradesh, India, were exposed to the highly toxic gas methyl isocyanate, in what is considered…","description":"On 3 December 1984, over 500,000 people in the vicinity of the Union Carbide India Limited pesticide plant in Bhopal, Madhya Pradesh, India, were exposed to the highly toxic gas methyl isocyanate, in what is considered the world's worst industrial disaster.*\n\nThe owner of the factory, Union Carbide India Limited (UCIL), was a publicly-traded company which was majority-owned and controlled by the UCC, while the remaining 49.1 percent stake was held by the Indian public and Indian government-controlled banks. In 1994, UCC sold its stake in UCIL to Eveready Industries India Limited (EIIL), which subsequently merged with McLeod Russel (India) Ltd. Eveready ended clean-up on the site in 1998, when it terminated its 99-year lease and turned over control of the site to the state government of Madhya Pradesh. Dow Chemical Company purchased UCC in 2001, seventeen years after the disaster.\n\nCivil and criminal cases filed in the United States against UCC and Warren Anderson, chief executive officer of the UCC at the time of the disaster, were dismissed and redirected to Indian courts on multiple occasions between 1986 and 2012, as the US courts focused on UCIL being a standalone entity of India. Civil and criminal cases were also filed in the District Court of Bhopal, India, involving UCC, UCIL, and Anderson. In June 2010, seven Indian nationals who were UCIL employees in 1984, including the former UCIL chairman Keshub Mahindra, were convicted in Bhopal of causing death by negligence and sentenced to two years' imprisonment and a fine of about $2,000 each, the maximum punishment allowed by Indian law. All were released on bail shortly after the verdict. An eighth former employee was also convicted, but died before the judgement was passed.\n\nThe UCIL factory was built in 1969 to produce the pesticide Sevin (UCC's brand name for carbaryl) using methyl isocyanate (MIC) as an intermediate. An MIC production plant was added to the UCIL site in 1979. The chemical process employed in the Bhopal plant had methylamine reacting with phosgene to form MIC, which was in turn reacted with 1-naphthol to form the final product, carbaryl. Another manufacturer, Bayer, also used this MIC-intermediate process at the chemical plant once owned by UCC at Institute, West Virginia in the United States.","evidence":"documented","universe":"bhopal-disaster","aliases":["Bhopal disaster"],"wikipedia_url":null,"image_url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Union_Carbide_pesticide_factory%2C_Bhopal%2C_India%2C_1985.jpg/180px-Union_Carbide_pesticide_factory%2C_Bhopal%2C_India%2C_1985.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","image_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bhopal_disaster","image_credit":"Fair-use identification thumbnail via Fandom. Bhopal disaster article text and identification images via Wikipedia; rights remain with original rights holders. Not free or redistributable artwork.","image_license":"fair_use","image_usage":"fair_use_identification","image_alt":"Identification thumbnail for Bhopal disaster"},"connections":[{"id":"6d28ec1f-2653-4103-80b4-43f4ab14462d","relationship":"part_of","title":"Part of","explanation":"Part of","strength":"0.9000","discovery_score":"0.8500","direction":"incoming","from_entity_id":"a0d3decd-184d-4e50-a589-fa08223088dd","to_entity_id":"c99961a5-053d-412f-89b4-681e26ee9908","entity_id":"a0d3decd-184d-4e50-a589-fa08223088dd","slug":"bhopal-express-film","name":"Bhopal Express","type":"topic","short_description":null,"evidence":"fiction","image_url":null,"path":"/bhopal-disaster/bhopal-express-film","kind":"topic","type_label":"Topic"},{"id":"8dccf607-b41d-4471-b95c-9f4d853eac37","relationship":"part_of","title":"Part of","explanation":"Part of","strength":"0.9000","discovery_score":"0.8500","direction":"incoming","from_entity_id":"782c6538-779c-4332-9ac1-7a9bc2913473","to_entity_id":"c99961a5-053d-412f-89b4-681e26ee9908","entity_id":"782c6538-779c-4332-9ac1-7a9bc2913473","slug":"animals-people","name":"Animal's People","type":"topic","short_description":null,"evidence":"fiction","image_url":null,"path":"/bhopal-disaster/animals-people","kind":"topic","type_label":"Topic"},{"id